# C Style Guidelines for BrogueMCP

## Overview

This document defines the C coding standards for the BrogueMCP project. Following these guidelines ensures consistent, maintainable, and efficient code across the codebase.

## Formatting

### Indentation and Spacing

- Use **4 spaces** for indentation, not tabs
- Maximum line length is **120 characters**
- Use one space after keywords (`if`, `for`, `while`, etc.)
- Use one space around operators (`=`, `+`, `*`, etc.)
- No space between function name and opening parenthesis
- One space after commas in argument lists

```c
// Good
if (condition) {
    doSomething();
}

// Bad
if(condition){
doSomething();
}
```

### Braces

- Opening braces go on the same line as the statement
- Closing braces go on their own line
- Always use braces, even for single-statement blocks

```c
// Good
if (condition) {
    doSomething();
} else {
    doSomethingElse();
}

// Bad
if (condition) doSomething();
else
    doSomethingElse();
```

### Function Declarations

- Return type on the same line as function name
- Parameters aligned if they span multiple lines
- Function names use camelCase

```c
int calculateDungeonLevel(int depth, int difficulty, 
                         DungeonParams params) {
    // Implementation
}
```

## Naming Conventions

### General Naming

- Use descriptive, meaningful names
- Avoid abbreviations unless they're universally understood
- Choose clarity over brevity

### Specific Conventions

- **Functions**: camelCase (e.g., `generateRoom`, `calculateDamage`)
- **Variables**: camelCase (e.g., `playerHealth`, `monsterCount`)
- **Constants**: ALL_CAPS with underscores (e.g., `MAX_DUNGEON_DEPTH`)
- **Typedefs**: PascalCase (e.g., `RoomType`, `EntityComponent`)
- **Enums**: enum name in PascalCase, values in ALL_CAPS
- **Struct members**: camelCase (e.g., `entity.position`, `room.width`)

```c
typedef struct Room {
    int width;
    int height;
    RoomType type;
    int monsterCount;
    Monster* monsters;
} Room;

// Enum example
typedef enum Direction {
    DIRECTION_NORTH,
    DIRECTION_EAST,
    DIRECTION_SOUTH,
    DIRECTION_WEST
} Direction;

#define MAX_ROOM_SIZE 20
#define MIN_CORRIDOR_LENGTH 3
```

## Code Organization

### File Structure

- Header files (.h) should contain declarations only
- Source files (.c) contain implementations
- Group related functions together
- Place helper functions near the functions that use them
- Order functions from high-level to low-level

### Header File Structure

```c
#ifndef DUNGEON_GENERATOR_H
#define DUNGEON_GENERATOR_H

// Includes from standard library
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

// Includes from project
#include "common.h"
#include "entity.h"

// Constants
#define MAX_ROOMS_PER_LEVEL 30

// Type definitions
typedef struct Room {
    // Members
} Room;

// Function declarations
Room* generateRoom(int width, int height);
bool connectRooms(Room* room1, Room* room2);

#endif // DUNGEON_GENERATOR_H
```

### Includes

- Include only what's necessary
- Use include guards in all header files
- Order includes: standard library, other libraries, project headers

## Memory Management

### Allocation Patterns

- Always check return values from `malloc` and other allocators
- Use consistent error handling for allocation failures
- Free memory in the reverse order of allocation when possible
- Consider memory pooling for frequently allocated/deallocated objects

```c
Entity* createEntity() {
    Entity* entity = (Entity*)malloc(sizeof(Entity));
    if (!entity) {
        logError("Failed to allocate entity");
        return NULL;
    }
    
    // Initialize entity
    entity->components = NULL;
    entity->componentCount = 0;
    
    return entity;
}

void destroyEntity(Entity* entity) {
    if (!entity) {
        return;
    }
    
    // Free components if they exist
    if (entity->components) {
        for (int i = 0; i < entity->componentCount; i++) {
            freeComponent(entity->components[i]);
        }
        free(entity->components);
    }
    
    free(entity);
}
```

### Roguelike-Specific Memory Patterns

- Use grid-based data structures for maps efficiently
- Consider using run-length encoding for large, sparse maps
- Implement object pooling for frequently created entities (e.g., projectiles)
- Use dirty rectangle tracking for efficient FOV/map updates

## Comments and Documentation

### Comment Style

- Use `//` for single-line comments
- Use `/* ... */` for multi-line comments
- Use Doxygen-style comments for function documentation

### Function Documentation

```c
/**
 * Generates a new dungeon level with the given parameters
 * 
 * @param depth The depth level in the dungeon
 * @param difficulty Scaling factor for dungeon challenges
 * @param params Additional generation parameters
 * @return Pointer to the new Level structure, or NULL on failure
 */
Level* generateDungeonLevel(int depth, int difficulty, DungeonParams params) {
    // Implementation
}
```

### Code Comments

- Comment complex algorithms
- Explain "why" rather than "what"
- Document non-obvious behavior
- Update comments when code changes

## Error Handling

- Use consistent error reporting
- Return NULL or error codes from functions that can fail
- Provide context in error messages
- Consider using a centralized logging system

```c
bool loadGameMap(const char* filename, GameMap* map) {
    FILE* file = fopen(filename, "rb");
    if (!file) {
        logError("Failed to open map file: %s", filename);
        return false;
    }
    
    // Read map data
    size_t read = fread(map->tiles, sizeof(Tile), map->width * map->height, file);
    if (read != map->width * map->height) {
        logError("Incomplete read from map file: %s", filename);
        fclose(file);
        return false;
    }
    
    fclose(file);
    return true;
}
```

## Roguelike-Specific Patterns

### Grid Coordinates

- Be consistent with coordinate systems (origin, axes, etc.)
- Document coordinate spaces clearly
- Consider using structs to represent positions

```c
typedef struct Position {
    int x;
    int y;
} Position;

// Using the position struct
void moveEntity(Entity* entity, Direction direction) {
    Position newPos = entity->position;
    
    switch (direction) {
        case DIRECTION_NORTH: newPos.y--; break;
        case DIRECTION_EAST:  newPos.x++; break;
        case DIRECTION_SOUTH: newPos.y++; break;
        case DIRECTION_WEST:  newPos.x--; break;
    }
    
    if (isPositionWalkable(newPos)) {
        entity->position = newPos;
    }
}
```

### Random Number Generation

- Use a consistent RNG approach
- Seed the RNG deterministically when needed
- Consider using a separate RNG stream for critical gameplay elements

```c
// Initialize RNG with seed
void initRNG(unsigned int seed) {
    srand(seed);
}

// Get random integer in range [min, max]
int randomInt(int min, int max) {
    return min + rand() % (max - min + 1);
}

// Get random float in range [0, 1]
float randomFloat() {
    return (float)rand() / RAND_MAX;
}

// Get random boolean with specified probability
bool randomChance(float probability) {
    return randomFloat() < probability;
}
```

## Performance Considerations

- Avoid unnecessary memory allocations in tight loops
- Use appropriate data structures for the task
- Consider spatial partitioning for entity/collision detection
- Profile and optimize critical paths

## Testing

- Write testable code
- Consider deterministic approaches for testing random elements
- Use seed values to reproduce specific procedural generation results

By following these guidelines, we ensure that BrogueMCP's codebase remains maintainable, efficient, and consistent across contributors.